# Target C-II — reciprocal `K_6` forces inherit a strict 120-degree turn-gap bound

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proved candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the six-point unit-force reciprocal equilibrium forced by a degree-pure minimal one-anchor `K_5` dual block  
**Depends on:** `target_c_one_anchor_k5_reciprocal_unit_force_2026-09-18.md`; source separation; completeness of the unit-contact graph  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The reciprocal reduction does not merely give five unit force vectors summing to zero at
each non-ground reciprocal point.  Those five force vectors are the oriented side
vectors of an actual bounded unit-contact pentagon.  Source separation therefore places
a strict upper bound on every cyclic angular gap between consecutive reciprocal force
directions.

For each non-ground reciprocal vertex `y_i`, write

\[
f_{ij}:={y_i-y_j\over\lambda_{ij}},
\qquad j\ne i.
\]

The five vectors `f_ij` have unit length, sum to zero, and, in cyclic angular order,
are exactly the five oriented side vectors of the physical pentagon `F_i`.

## 1. Every exterior turn is strictly below 120 degrees

Take two consecutive oriented side vectors `u,v` on one physical pentagon.  Let `theta`
be their exterior turning angle.  Strict convexity gives

\[
0<\theta<\pi.
\]

The vector `u+v` joins the two nonconsecutive endpoints across the common pentagon
vertex.  Those two source points have distance at least one.  Equality is impossible:
if `|u+v|=1`, that segment is another unit contact lying inside the strict convex face,
so the complete unit-contact graph would contain a chord subdividing the face.
Therefore

\[
|u+v|>1.
\]

Since `|u|=|v|=1`,

\[
|u+v|=2\cos(\theta/2),
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{\theta<{2\pi\over3}.}
\tag{1.1}
\]

Applying this to the five reciprocal force vectors gives:

\[
\boxed{
\text{at every }y_i\ (i=1,\ldots,5),
\text{ every cyclic gap between consecutive }f_{ij}\text{ is }<120^\circ.
}
\tag{1.2}
\]

This is stronger than the equilibrium statement `sum_j f_ij=0`, which by itself only
prevents all five directions from lying in one open semicircle.

## 2. Consequence for reciprocal convex-hull angles

Suppose a non-ground reciprocal point `y_i` is a vertex of the convex hull of
`{y_0,...,y_5}` and let `phi_i` be its hull interior angle.  The raw directions

\[
{y_i-y_j\over|y_i-y_j|}
\]

to the other five reciprocal points lie in one closed cone of angular width `phi_i`.
Multiplying individual directions by the edge signs `sgn(lambda_ij)` either leaves a
direction in that cone or rotates it by `pi`.  Thus all five force directions lie in
the union of two opposite cones, each of width `phi_i`.

The two complementary angular gaps between those cones each have width

\[
\pi-\phi_i.
\]

No force direction can lie inside those open gaps.  By (1.2) the largest cyclic empty
gap is strictly below `2pi/3`.  Therefore

\[
\pi-\phi_i<{2\pi\over3},
\]

so

\[
\boxed{\phi_i>{\pi\over3}.}
\tag{2.1}
\]

Every non-ground reciprocal hull vertex therefore has interior angle strictly greater
than 60 degrees.

An immediate combinatorial consequence is that if the reciprocal convex hull is a
triangle, then the ground point `y_0=0` must be one of its three vertices.  A triangle
whose three vertices were all among `y_1,...,y_5` would have all three interior angles
strictly greater than 60 degrees, impossible because they sum to 180 degrees.

This does not yet exclude a reciprocal triangle containing `y_0`, nor reciprocal hulls
with four, five, or six vertices.  It does remove one full order-type class from the
finite `K_6` target and supplies a source-derived strict angular inequality for future
classification or exact computation.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** transferred the source-separation condition on each physical contact
pentagon to the reciprocal `K_6`, proving a strict `120`-degree maximum force-direction
gap and the reciprocal hull-angle bound `phi_i>60` degrees at every non-ground hull
vertex.  C-II remains open.